text:
jordan: Created a quick PR to expand the Troubleshooting > Rate limits section. Adds a short 429 vs timeout (504) diff, Retry-After handling, and a minimal JS/Py snippet for polite fallback. Need a quick technical review.

anya: Thanks. Two things: make sure we call out streaming partials on 429s and show the header name variations (Retry-After vs x-retry-after). Also rename `retry_policy` to `backoff_strategy` in the examples.

phil: I'll clean the JS snippet and add AbortController example for client-side timeouts so folks don't mix up 504s and client cancels.

jordan: Draft JS snippet I put in PR: ```js
// check Retry-After header then fallback
const retry = parseInt(resp.headers['retry-after'] || resp.headers['x-retry-after'] || '0', 10);
if (retry > 0) await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, retry * 1000));
// fallback to smaller model
await client.generate({ model: 'redwood-medium', messages: ctx });
```

anya: Short migration note added: "\"backoff_strategy\": { \"type\": \"exponential\", \"base_ms\": 250, \"max_ms\": 8000, \"jitter\": true }". Also recommend dropping oldest messages when trimming context.

phil: I pushed an AbortController snippet and a one-line streaming note: "On 429 during streaming, you may get partial frames; check headers and retry-after before resuming."

docs-bot: PR 7460: CI passed :white_check_mark: (docs build + linkcheck)

anya: LGTM. Small nit: use triple-backtick fences with language hint for both snippets.

jordan: Fixed fences and merged. I'll add a changelog blurb after lunch.

phil: Great, will watch for any follow-ups from support.
